// KernRift bare-metal output provider — 16550 / NS8250 UART, x86_64 COM1.
//
//   import "std/uart_16550.kr"
//   krc --arch=x86_64 --target=none prog.kr -o prog.img
//
// Under `--target=none` there is no kernel, so `write` is not a syscall and
// the compiler refuses the builtin (src/ir.kr). This module supplies the
// replacement through `@builtin_override`: the annotation registers the name
// so every builtin-dispatch chain (IR lowering and both legacy backends)
// skips its own `write` and emits a normal call to the body below.
//
// `print`, `println`, `print_str` and `println_str` are lowered to calls of
// THIS function too when `--target=none` is in effect (src/ir.kr) — they are
// separate builtins that emitted their own syscall, so without that routing
// an override named `write` would leave all four still refusing. One provider
// therefore serves all five; a program needs no changes beyond the import.
//
// A PROVIDER, NOT A DRIVER. The 16550 itself lives in `std/serial.kr`; this
// file is the shim that publishes it as the compiler's `write`. It used to
// carry its own port I/O and its own copy of the register map, so the stdlib
// held two independent 16550 implementations that could drift apart. The
// duplicates are gone: `uart16550_outb` and `uart16550_inb` were deleted in
// favour of `outb`/`inb` from `std/x86.kr` (reached through `serial.kr`), and
// the `UART16550_IER`/`_FCR`/`_LCR`/`_MCR`/`_LSR`/`_LSR_THRE` offsets in
// favour of `serial.kr`'s `UART_*` set, which this import brings into scope.
// `UART16550_BASE` stays, because which port to use is a real knob.
//
// MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH std/console.kr, BY DESIGN. Both are print
// providers, so both define `write` with `@builtin_override` and importing
// the two together is
//
//     error: redefinition of function 'write'
//
// naming a line in a stdlib file you did not write, which is a confusing way
// to find out. Choose one:
//
//   * `std/uart_16550.kr` — serial only. The right choice when the machine
//     has no display, when only the serial log is captured, or when the VGA
//     text buffer is not wanted (`-vga none`).
//   * `std/console.kr` — serial AND the VGA text screen, plus PS/2 keyboard
//     input.
//
// Importing `std/serial.kr` alongside either is fine, and normal: `serial.kr`
// defines no `write` of its own.
//
// WHY THIS IS LIBRARY CODE. A UART base address is a *board* fact, not a
// compiler fact. 0x3F8 is COM1 on a PC-compatible machine and on QEMU's
// `-serial` device; it is wrong on anything else. Baking it into codegen
// would make the compiler wrong on any other board and unoverridable without
// patching it. Copy this file, change UART16550_BASE, keep the annotation.
//
// x86_64 ONLY: the 16550 lives behind port I/O (`in`/`out`), which exists on
// no other architecture in this tree. arm64 gets std/uart_pl011.kr. Importing
// both into one program is a duplicate definition of `write` and is rejected.

import "serial.kr"

// COM1. COM2 is 0x2F8, COM3 0x3E8, COM4 0x2E8. Set this before calling
// uart16550_init() to drive a different port.
static uint64 UART16550_BASE = 0x3F8

// 115200 baud, 8N1, FIFOs on.
//
// CALL THIS. An earlier version of this comment said firmware "usually" leaves
// COM1 usable and QEMU "always" does, so `write` works without it. That was an
// UNOBSERVED RUNTIME CLAIM: nothing in this sub-project had ever produced a
// character on a UART, on silicon or under an emulator, so "works without
// this" was not something anyone here had seen. (It has since been seen under
// QEMU — the boot gate's sentinels print through this provider — but that is
// QEMU, and what real firmware leaves configured is still exactly the thing
// that has not been checked.)
fn uart16550_init() {
    serial_init_port(UART16550_BASE, UART_DIVISOR_115200)
}

// One byte, after waiting for the transmitter to be ready. No LF-to-CRLF
// translation — this is the raw byte, deliberately, so `write` stays a byte
// pipe. `serial_putc` is the translating one if you want it.
fn uart16550_putc(uint64 c) {
    serial_putc_port(UART16550_BASE, c & 0xFF)
}

// The provider. `fd` is accepted and ignored: a board with one UART has one
// output, and both stdout (1) and stderr (2) are that UART. Returns the byte
// count, matching the builtin's contract, so callers that check the return
// value keep working.
//
// The return value is the count actually pushed, which is always `len` here
// because uart16550_putc blocks until the transmitter accepts the byte.
@builtin_override
fn write(uint64 _fd, uint64 buf, uint64 len) -> uint64 {
    uint64 i = 0
    while i < len {
        uart16550_putc(load8(buf + i))
        i = i + 1
    }
    return len
}
